Title: why std::pair&lt;int[N], int[N]&gt; is not allowed in C++?.
Body: <p>Why the C++ code below can't be compiled?</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;utility&gt;

int main() {
    int x[6];
    int y[6];
    std::pair&lt;int[6], int[6]&gt; a(x, y);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>For example MSVC gives the following error:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>error C2661: 'std::pair&lt;int [6],int [6]&gt;::pair': no overloaded function takes 2 argument</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thanks for the comments that using C++ alternatives to construct arrays, but I also want to know the reason that the code can't be compiled.</p>

Answers:
```cpp
#include <utility>

int main() {
    int x[6];
    int y[6];
    std::pair<int*, int*> a(x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

This code is allowed because `std::pair` in C++ takes two arguments of the same type, not different types. The syntax `std::pair<int[6], int[6]>` is not valid in C++. However, you can create an array of pairs instead:

```cpp
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int x[6];
    int y[6];
    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> a(x, x+6);
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, `a` is a vector of pairs, where each pair contains an element from `x` and an element from `y`.

