Title: Why can I declare array with a constant size in some cases and not others?.
Body: <p>Im trying to create a struct that includes a 2d array along with some member functions.</p>
<p>When I write:</p>
<pre><code>struct Board {
    const int BOARDSIZE = 8;

    bool data[BOARDSIZE][BOARDSIZE];
};
</code></pre>
<p>I get &quot;a nonstatic member reference must be relative to a specific object.&quot; However, when I write:</p>
<pre><code>const int BOARDSIZE = 8;

struct Board {
    bool data[BOARDSIZE][BOARDSIZE];
};
</code></pre>
<p>or:</p>
<pre><code>struct Board {
    bool data[8][8];
};
</code></pre>
<p>there is no error. I've searched around and cant figure out why it wont accept one and not the other. I'd rather not declare BOARDSIZE as a global variable because I dont want it just floating around in a large project but I also dont want to have to write 8 every time a member function of the struct needs the board size for flexability's sake down the line.</p>
<p>Is there a way to have a local variable that represents the size of the board?</p>

Answers:
```python
class Board:
    def __init__(self):
        self.BOARDSIZE = 8
        self.data = [[False for _ in range(self.BOARDSIZE)] for _ in range(self.BOARDSIZE)]

    def print_board(self):
        for row in self.data:
            print(row)

    def set_board(self, row, col, value):
        self.data[row][col] = value

# Usage
board = Board()
board.print_board()
board.set_board(3, 3, True)
board.print_board()
```

def can_form_palindrome(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n - 1):
        count = {}
        for j in range(i, n):
            temp = arr[j]
            for char in temp:
                if char in count:
                    count[char] += 1
                else:
                    count[char] = 1
            if j > i:
                temp = arr[i]
                for char in temp:
                    if char in count:
                        count[char] -= 1
                    else:
                        return False
    for char in count:
        if count[char] % 2 != 0:
            return False
    return True

arr = ["aa", "bab", "cde", "aba", "ab"]
print(can_form_palindrome(arr))assistant

This function works by iterating over all pairs of consecutive elements in the array. For each pair, it counts the occurrences of each character in both elements. If the characters are different, it moves the rightmost character of the first element to the leftmost position in the second element and vice versa. If the characters are the same, it does nothing. After processing all pairs, it checks if the count of each character is even. If any character has an odd count, it means that character cannot be moved to make the array palindromic, so the function returns False. If all characters have even counts, the function returns True.

