Questions:
Title: C/C++ warn or prohibit literal string concatenation.
Body: <p>Is there a way to warn or prohibit literal string concatenations such as:</p>
<pre><code>const char *a = &quot;foo&quot; &quot; bar&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>I spent hours finding a bug in a big static array that had</p>
<pre><code>const char * a[] = {&quot;foo&quot; &quot;bar&quot;};
</code></pre>
<p>instead of</p>
<pre><code>const char * a[] = {&quot;foo&quot;, &quot;bar&quot;};
</code></pre>

Answers:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void check_concat(const std::string& str) {
    if (str.find(' ') != std::string::npos) {
        std::cerr << "Warning: Literal string concatenation detected." << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    const char *a = "foo" "bar";
    check_concat(a);

    const char *b[] = {"foo", "bar"};
    check_concat(b[0]);

    return 0;
}
```

```csharp
// In C#, triple double quotes are used for multiline strings.
// They provide a way to write multi-line strings without having to escape the quotes.

// Single double quotes with the @ symbol are used for verbatim strings, 
// which are useful for working with file paths, regular expressions, etc.
```

```python
def replace_chars(strings):
    replacements = {
        'Y': 'pY',
        'S': 'pS',
        'T': 'pT'
    }

    result = []
    for s in strings:
        new_s = ''
        for char in s:
            if char in replacements:
                new_s += replacements[char]
            else:
                new_s += char
        result.append(new_s)

    return result

strings = ["YSAHEEHHYDK", "HEHISSDYAGK", "TFAHTESHISK", "ISLGEHEGGGK", 
           "LSSGYDGTSYK", "FGTGTYAGGEK", "VGASTGYSGLK", "TASGVGGFSTK", 
           "SYASDFGSSAK", "LYSYYSSTESK"]
print(replace_chars(strings))
```

