Title: How to design a base class that needs to join a thread in its destructor, which operates on the same class instance?.
Body: <p>I just got a juicy race condition. Consider the following classes:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;atomic&gt;
#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;thread&gt;

class A
{
    std::thread th;
    std::atomic_bool stop = false;

  public:
    A() = default;
    A(const A &amp;) = delete;
    A &amp;operator=(const A &amp;) = delete;

    ~A()
    {
        stop.store(true);
        th.join();
    }

    virtual void Step() = 0;

    void Start()
    {
        th = std::thread([this]
        {
            while (true)
            {
                if (stop.load())
                    return;

                // Just to help reproduce the race condition.
                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(50));

                Step();
            }
        });
    }
};

struct B : A
{
    void Step() override
    {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Step!&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    B b;

    b.Start();

    // Just to help reproduce the race condition.
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(110));
}
</code></pre>
<p>This crashes due to a pure virtual function call (if we ignore the data race UB).</p>
<p>Right before we enter the body of <code>~A()</code>, before we can stop the thread, the vtable pointer is changed to point to <code>A</code>'s vtable, so the next call of <code>Step()</code> crashes, now being a pure virtual call.</p>
<p>This can be fixed by stopping the thread in <code>~B()</code>, but that means every derived class must remember to do it, or bad things will happen.</p>
<p>I wish I could design <code>A</code> to take care of this by itself. Is it possible?</p>

Answers:
```python
import threading

class BaseClass:
    def __init__(self):
        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._worker)

    def _worker(self):
        while not self._stop.is_set():
            # Just to help reproduce the race condition.
            import time
            time.sleep(0.05)
            self.step()

    def start(self):
        self._thread.start()

    def stop(self):
        self._stop.set()

    def step(self):
        # This method must be implemented by derived classes
        pass

class DerivedClass(BaseClass):
    def step(self):
        print("Step!")

def main():
    base = DerivedClass()
    base.start()

    # Just to help reproduce the race condition.
    import time
    time.sleep(1.1)

    base.stop()

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

This code achieves the same result as your C++ code. The `BaseClass` class manages the thread and stops it when the `stop` method is called. The `step` method is declared as an abstract method, which must be implemented by derived classes. In this example, we use the `DerivedClass` to demonstrate how to use the `BaseClass`. The `stop` method is called after a short delay to simulate the race condition.

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex, Cell};

fn main() {
    let data = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Cell::new(0.0)));
    let mut handles = vec![];

    for _ in 0..10 {
        let data_clone = Arc::clone(&data);
        let cell = Arc::clone(&data_clone);
        let mut handle = std::thread::spawn(move || {
            let mut data_inner = cell.get_mut();
            *data_inner += 1.0;
        });
        handles.push(handle);
    }

    for handle in handles {
        handle.join().unwrap();
    }

    println!("{}", *data.lock().unwrap().get());
}
```

```python
# This Python code will not directly answer the question as it's a Java-related issue.
# However, it can be used as a reference to understand how to detach a thread in Java using JNI.

import java.lang.Thread;
import java.lang.ThreadGroup;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

class TestThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        // Your native code here
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a new thread
        TestThread thread = new TestThread();

        // Attach the native thread to the JVM
        // Note: This is not directly equivalent to calling AttachCurrentThread in Java
        //       but it shows how to create and detach a native thread using JNI
        thread.start();
        ThreadGroup group = Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup();
        Method detachMethod = Thread.class.getDeclaredMethod("detachThread", Thread.class);
        detachMethod.setAccessible(true);
        detachMethod.invoke(group, thread);
    }
}
```

