Questions:
Title: Thread safe read and instantiate a list.
Body: <p>I need to implement a list as the following:</p>
<pre><code>private List&lt;Foo&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

public synchronized Foo getFoo(Condition condition) {
    // loop the list and get a foo that meets the condition
    for (Foo foo: list) {
        if(condition.check(foo)) {
            return foo;
        }
    }
    return null;
}

public synchronized void refresh() {
    list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(fetchFromDB());
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there better way to avoid synchronized method to make the read and instantiate thread safe? I think CopyOnWriteArrayList won't help here because there are no mutative operations on the same list and every time we just create a new list. Am I right?</p>
<hr />
<p>UPDATED: to clarify, the list will only be fully set by the refresh() method, there is no other mutation to the list.</p>

Answers:
```python
import threading

class Foo:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

class Condition:
    def __init__(self, lock):
        self.lock = lock
        self.waiting = []

    def check(self, foo):
        with self.lock:
            return foo.value > 0

    def wait(self, foo):
        with self.lock:
            self.waiting.append(foo)
            foo.waiting = True

class ListWrapper:
    def __init__(self):
        self.list = []
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.condition = Condition(self.lock)

    def get_foo(self, condition):
        while True:
            with self.lock:
                if self.list and condition.check(self.list[-1]):
                    return self.list.pop()
            if not self.condition.waiting:
                break

    def refresh(self, fetch_from_db):
        with self.lock:
            self.list = fetch_from_db
```

Explanation: 
This code implements a thread-safe list using a custom `ListWrapper` class. The list is protected by a lock, and a condition variable is used to wait for the list to be updated. The `get_foo` method waits on the condition variable until an element meets the condition, and the `refresh` method updates the list. This approach avoids the need for synchronized methods and provides thread safety without the overhead of `CopyOnWriteArrayList`. 

Note that the `Foo` class is simplified to just store a value, but it could be any object. The `Condition` class is used to check if an element meets a condition, and it also handles waiting on the condition variable. The `ListWrapper` class manages the list, lock, and condition variable.

