Answers:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  mode: 'production',
  target: 'node',
  entry: './index.js',
  output: {
    filename: 'bundle.js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist')
  },
  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\.js$/,
        exclude: /node_modules/,
        use: {
          loader: 'babel-loader',
          options: {
            presets:  [
              ['@babel/preset-env', {
                targets: {
                  node: 'current'
                }
              }]
            ],
          },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  resolve: {
    extensions: ['.js', '.ts'],
    alias: {
      './src': path.resolve(__dirname, 'src'),
      './routes': path.resolve(__dirname, 'src/routes'),
    }
  }
};
```

The error message you're seeing is due to the fact that the `sourceType` option in the `babel-loader` configuration is set to `'module'`, but the file you're trying to import is not a JavaScript module.

In your case, the issue is likely due to the fact that you're trying to import `lodash` in your `index.js` file, but you've only configured the `babel-loader` to handle `.js` files, not CommonJS modules.

To fix this issue, you can modify your `webpack.config.js` file to include the `@babel/preset-env` preset with the `targets` option set to `{ node: 'current' }`, which will allow Babel to compile the current Node.js version.

Additionally, you can add the `resolve` option to your `webpack.config.js` file to configure the alias for the `src` and `routes` directories.

Also, make sure that your `package.json` file has the following script:

```json
"scripts": {
  "build": "webpack"
}
```

And run the following command in your terminal:

```bash
npm run build
```

This will compile your code using the `babel-loader` and generate a bundle for your Node.js application.

```java
@XmlRootElement(name = "accountClientIdentifier")
@XmlType(propOrder = {"accountId", "name", "value", "expires"})
@JsonInclude(JsonInclude.Include.NON_NULL)
public class AccountClientIdentifierEntity {

    public static void registerToContextProvider() {
        try {
            CustomContextProvider.registerClass(AccountClientIdentifierEntity.class);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            Log.error("Could not register AccountClientIdentifierEntity: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    /** The username. */
    private String accountId;

    /** The name. */
    private String name;

    /** The value. */
    private String value;

    /** expires. */
    private Instant expires;

    public AccountClientIdentifierEntity() {}

    public AccountClientIdentifierEntity(String accountId, String name, String value) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.name = name;
        this.value = value;
        this.expires = null; // Initialize expires to null
    }

    public Instant getExpires() {
        return expires;
    }

    public void setExpires(Instant expires) {
        this.expires = expires;
    }
}
```

The problem is that when you're parsing the JSON, the `expires` field is being parsed as an integer, but it's supposed to be an `Instant`. The `NoSuchMethodError` is being thrown because the `Instant` class doesn't have a constructor that takes an integer, and the Java XML Binding (JAXB) is trying to create an instance of `Instant` with an integer value.

One way to fix this is to initialize the `expires` field to `null` in the constructor, as shown in the updated code. This will prevent the `NoSuchMethodError` from being thrown when the `expires` field is parsed as an integer.

Additionally, you may want to consider adding a check in the `setExpires` method to ensure that the `expires` field is not set to an integer value. This can be done by casting the value to an `Instant` and checking if it's `null`. If it's not `null`, you can set the `expires` field to that value.

```java
public void setExpires(Instant expires) {
    if (expires != null) {
        this.expires = expires;
    } else {
        this.expires = null;
    }
}
```

This will prevent the `NoSuchMethodError` from being thrown and ensure that the `expires` field is always set to an `Instant` value.

