&quot;Field 2&quot;
&quot;Field 3, Line 1&quot;
&quot;Field 1, Line 2&quot;
&quot;Field 2&quot;
&quot;Field 3, Line 2&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>I tried this:</p>
<pre><code>mlr --csv -N  --ofs lf --quote-all cat file
</code></pre>
<p>Which produces no output.</p>
<p>As a workaround, I can do this:</p>
<pre><code>mlr --csv -N  --ofs pipe --quote-all cat file | sed 's/&quot;\|&quot;/&quot;\n&quot;/g'
</code></pre>
<p>Or use ruby:</p>
<pre><code>ruby -r csv -e 'CSV.parse($&lt;.read).flatten.each{|r| puts [r].to_csv(force_quotes: true)}' file
</code></pre>
<p>But it does feel that I should be able to just look at a csv file one field at a time (and ignore records) with Miller?</p>

Answers:
import copy
from collections import Counter, defaultdict

class MyCounter(Counter):
    def __init__(self, foo):
        self.foo = foo

    def __deepcopy__(self, memo):
        return type(self)(self._deepcopy_dict(memo))

    def _deepcopy_dict(self, memo):
        d = defaultdict(int)
        for k, v in self.items():
            if isinstance(v, dict):
                d[k] = copy.deepcopy(v, memo)
            elif isinstance(v, Counter):
                d[k] = copy.deepcopy(v, memo)
            else:
                d[k] = v
        return dict(d)


class MyDict(dict):
    def __init__(self, foo):
        self.foo = foo

    def __deepcopy__(self, memo):
        return type(self)(self._deepcopy_dict(memo))

    def _deepcopy_dict(self, memo):
        d = defaultdict(int)
        for k, v in self.items():
            if isinstance(v, dict):
                d[k] = copy.deepcopy(v, memo)
            elif isinstance(v, Counter):
                d[k] = copy.deepcopy(v, memo)
            else:
                d[k] = v
        return dict(d)

