Title: Why can&#39;t private class fields be deleted?.
Body: <p>It seems it's not possible to delete <code>#</code>private class fields. The syntax doesn't allow it.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>class Example {
    #privatefield = 'some_value';

    deleteField() {
        delete this.#privatefield;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>Uncaught SyntaxError: Private fields can not be deleted
</code></pre>
<p>Does anyone know why? What is the rationale behind allowing deletion of normal properties but not of private class fields?</p>

Answers:
class Example {
    #privatefield = 'some_value';

    def deleteField(self):
        del self.#privatefield
}

try:
    example = Example()
    example.deleteField()
except AttributeError:
    print("Cannot delete private field")

```python
class NewClass:
    def __init__(self):
        self.instructions = []
        self.annotations = []

        self.instructions.append([0, 1, 2])
        self.instructions.append([5, 6, 7, 8])
        self.instructions.append([12, 13])

        self.annotations.append([3, 4])
        self.annotations.append([9, 10, 11])
        self.annotations.append([14, 15, 16])

    def __getitem__(self, ind):
        if ind >= 0:
            instructions_idx = ind % len(self.instructions)
            annotations_idx = ind // len(self.instructions)
        else:
            instructions_idx = len(self.instructions) - 1 - abs(ind) % len(self.instructions)
            annotations_idx = len(self.instructions) - 1 - abs(ind) // len(self.instructions)

        if instructions_idx < len(self.instructions) and annotations_idx < len(self.annotations):
            return self.instructions[instructions_idx][annotations_idx]
        else:
            raise IndexError('index out of range')
```

Questions:
Title: Why is JavaScript private method accessible from console.log.
Body: <p>I wrote a simple code:</p>
<pre><code>const secure = new class {
    #privateProperty = 4;
    #privateMethod() {
        console.log( 'The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class' );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If it is immediately followed by the syntax below</p>
<p><code>secure.#privateMethod();</code></p>
<p>I get an error which says <code>Uncaught SyntaxError: Private field '#privateMethod' must be declared in an enclosing class</code></p>
<p>However, If I don't immediately call the <code>secure.#privateMethod()</code> and then go to developer tool - console and write the syntax there, it outputs:</p>
<p><code>The property 4 should not be accessible outside this class</code></p>
<p>Is there a special reason why this is happening?</p>

Answers:
```javascript
class Secure {
    #privateProperty = 4;

    #privateMethod() {
        console.log('The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class');
    }
}

const secure = new Secure();
secure.#privateMethod();
```

The reason for this behavior lies in how JavaScript handles private fields and methods. In JavaScript, private fields and methods are not enforced by the language itself. Instead, they rely on the surrounding class to maintain their encapsulation.

When you try to call `secure.#privateMethod()` immediately after creating the `secure` object, it throws an error because the `#privateMethod` is not yet defined when it's called. This is because the JavaScript engine only checks for the existence of a property or method at runtime, not at compile time.

However, when you access `#privateMethod` through the console, it's treated as a property, not a method. This is because the JavaScript engine can access the property at runtime, allowing it to evaluate the expression `The property 4 should not be accessible outside this class` correctly.

To achieve similar behavior in a more controlled way, you can use a wrapper function to call the private method:

```javascript
class Secure {
    #privateProperty = 4;

    #privateMethod() {
        console.log('The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class');
    }
}

const secure = new Secure();

function callPrivateMethod() {
    secure.#privateMethod();
}

callPrivateMethod();
```

