Questions:
Title: How to create endpoint with different response content types depending on accept header in Java with Micronaut.
Body: <p>I am having a play with Micronaut, and want to create an endpoint that returns either <code>text/plain</code> or <code>text/html</code>, depending on the Accept header in the request.</p>
<p>I found two ways to do this:</p>
<ol>
<li>create two methods for the same endpoint with different <em>produces</em> attributes,</li>
<li>handle it all in a single method and that evaluates the accept header and use logic in my own code to return the right content type.</li>
</ol>
<p>I like the first way best, because it's very clean, and requires no logic from me:</p>
<pre><code>    @Get(uri=&quot;/&quot;, produces=&quot;text/html&quot;)
    public String indexHtml() {
        return &quot;&quot;&quot;
            &lt;html&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta charset=\&quot;utf-8\&quot;&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
            &lt;body&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;Demo service&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;;
    }
    @Get(uri=&quot;/&quot;, produces=&quot;text/plain&quot;)
    public String indexText() {
        return &quot;Demo service\r\n&quot;;
    }

</code></pre>
<p>This works well when an accept header is in the request. However, when there is no accept header, I get an error response <code>400 Bad Request</code> with a message <code>&quot;More than 1 route matched the incoming request. The following routes matched /demo: GET - /demo, GET - /demo&quot;</code></p>
<p><strong>Question 1</strong>
Is there a way to provide a 3rd endpoint as a fallback, or to designate one of these methods as the default?</p>
<p>The second way to do it, resulted in a method that I don't find very elegant:</p>
<pre><code>    @Get(uri=&quot;/&quot;, produces={MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN, MediaType.TEXT_HTML})
    public String index(HttpRequest&lt;?&gt; request) {
        Collection&lt;MediaType&gt; mediaTypes = request.accept();
        Iterator&lt;MediaType&gt; it = mediaTypes.iterator();
        if (it.hasNext()) {
            MediaType mediaType = it.next();
            if (MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN_TYPE.equals(mediaType))
                    return &quot;Demo service\r\n&quot;;
            if (MediaType.TEXT_HTML_TYPE.equals(mediaType))
                    return &quot;&quot;&quot;
                        &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
                        &lt;html&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta charset=\&quot;utf-8\&quot;&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
                        &lt;body&gt;
                        &lt;h1&gt;Demo service&lt;/h1&gt;
                        &lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;;
        }
        return &quot;&quot;&quot;
            {
                &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Demo service&quot;,
            }&quot;&quot;&quot;;
    }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Question 2</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>If it's really necessary to handle this in a single method, is it indeed done by fetching the accept header and evaluating that, or should it be done differently?</li>
</ul>

Answers:
```python
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
from flask_httpauth import HTTPTokenAuth

app = Flask(__name__)
auth = HTTPTokenAuth(scheme='Bearer')

# Define a dictionary to map accept headers to content types
accept_mapping = {
    'text/plain': 'text/plain',
    'text/html': 'text/html'
}

# Define a decorator to determine the content type based on the accept header
def get_content_type():
    accept_header = request.accept_mimetypes.best_match(accept_mapping.keys())
    return accept_header or 'text/plain'

# Define the endpoint with a fallback to text/plain
@app.route('/', methods=['GET'])
@auth.login_required
def index():
    content_type = get_content_type()
    return jsonify({'title': 'Demo service'})

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

```python
from spyne import Application, rpc, ServiceBase, Integer, Double
from spyne.protocol.soap import Soap11
from spyne.server.wsgi import WsgiApplication
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
import logging

class CalculatorService(ServiceBase):
    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Integer)
    def addition(ctx, a, b):
        return a + b

    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Integer)
    def subtraction(ctx, a, b):
        return a - b

    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Integer)
    def multiplication(ctx, a, b):
        return a * b

    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Double)
    def division(ctx, a, b):
        return a / b


application = Application([CalculatorService], 'services.calculator.soap',
                          in_protocol=Soap11(validator='lxml'),
                          out_protocol=Soap11())

wsgi_application = WsgiApplication(application)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)
    logging.getLogger('spyne.protocol.xml').setLevel(logging.INFO)

    logging.info("listening to http://127.0.0.1:8000")
    logging.info("wsdl is at: http://127.0.0.1:8000/?wsdl")

    app = Flask(__name__)
    app.wsgi_app = wsgi_application

    @app.route('/add', methods=['POST'])
    def add():
        try:
            a = int(request.form['a'])
            b = int(request.form['b'])
            return jsonify({'result': a + b})
        except (KeyError, ValueError):
            return jsonify({'error': 'Invalid input'}), 400

    @app.route('/subtract', methods=['POST'])
    def subtract():
        try:
            a = int(request.form['a'])
            b = int(request.form['b'])
            return jsonify({'result': a - b})
        except (KeyError, ValueError):
            return jsonify({'error': 'Invalid input'}), 400

    @app.route('/multiply', methods=['POST'])
    def multiply():
        try:
            a = int(request.form['a'])
            b = int(request.form['b'])
            return jsonify({'result': a * b})
        except (KeyError, ValueError):
            return jsonify({'error': 'Invalid input'}), 400

    @app.route('/divide', methods=['POST'])
    def divide():
        try:
            a = int(request.form['a'])
            b = int(request.form['b'])
            if b == 0:
                return jsonify({'error': 'Cannot divide by zero'}), 400
            return jsonify({'result': a / b})
        except (KeyError, ValueError):
            return jsonify({'error': 'Invalid input'}), 400

    app.run(host='127.0.0.1', port=8000)
```

Questions:
Title: How to customize JAX-WS RuntimeException responses?.
Body: <p>I am trying to customize the fault responses for <code>RuntimeException</code>s thrown before reaching to the <code>WebMethod</code>, like invalid xml body or wrong method name  for a JAX-WS service.
I tried to use a handler with <code>@HandlerChain</code> but the response is sent before processed by the handler.</p>
<p>Here is an example. Lets say i have a method copyTool method in my ws but client sent below request. I want to be able to customize the response so that i can change the faultString or return some custom faultCode.</p>
<p><strong>Input</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;soapenv:Envelope xmlns:soapenv=&quot;http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/&quot;
xmlns:cor=&quot;http://acme.com/&quot;&gt;
   &lt;soapenv:Header/&gt;
   &lt;soapenv:Body&gt;
      &lt;cor:copyTool&gt;
       &lt;toolNumber&gt;some Text instead of Integer&lt;/toolNumber&gt;
      &lt;/cor:copyTool&gt;
   &lt;/soapenv:Body&gt;
&lt;/soapenv:Envelope&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Output</strong></p>
<pre><code>    &lt;S:Envelope xmlns:S=&quot;http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/&quot;&gt;
   &lt;S:Body&gt;
      &lt;ns0:Fault xmlns:ns0=&quot;http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/&quot; xmlns:ns1=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2003/05/soap-envelope&quot;&gt;
         &lt;faultcode&gt;ns0:Server&lt;/faultcode&gt;
         &lt;faultstring&gt;Exception Description: The object [some Text instead of Integer], of class [class java.lang.String], from mapping [org.eclipse.persistence.oxm.mappings.XMLDirectMapping[toolNumber--&gt;toolNumber/text()]] with descriptor [XMLDescriptor(com.acme --&gt; [DatabaseTable(ns0:copyTool)])], could not be converted to [class java.lang.Integer].
Internal Exception: java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: &quot;some Text instead of Integer&quot;&lt;/faultstring&gt;
      &lt;/ns0:Fault&gt;
   &lt;/S:Body&gt;
&lt;/S:Envelope&gt;
</code></pre>

Answers:
```python
from xml.etree import ElementTree as ET
from jaxws import Fault

class CustomFaultHandler:
    def __init__(self):
        self.fault_codes = {}

    def handle_fault(self, fault):
        fault_code = 'custom fault code'
        fault_string = 'Custom fault string'
        fault_code_map = {'custom fault code': 'ns0:Server'}

        fault.setFaultCode(fault_code_map.get(fault_code, fault_code))
        fault.setFaultString(fault_string)

        return fault

def parse_xml(xml_string):
    root = ET.fromstring(xml_string)
    tool_number = root.find('.//toolNumber').text
    try:
        tool_number = int(tool_number)
    except ValueError:
        tool_number = None
    return tool_number

def generate_custom_fault_response(tool_number):
    if tool_number is None:
        custom_fault_handler = CustomFaultHandler()
        fault = Fault(fault_code='custom fault code', fault_string='Custom fault string')
        return ET.Element('S:Envelope')
        # return custom_fault_handler.handle_fault(fault)
    else:
        return ET.Element('S:Envelope')

# Example usage
input_xml = '<soapenv:Envelope xmlns:soapenv="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/" xmlns:cor="http://acme.com/"><soapenv:Header/<soapenv:Body><cor:copyTool><toolNumber>some Text instead of Integer</toolNumber></cor:copyTool></soapenv:Body></soapenv:Envelope>'
tool_number = parse_xml(input_xml)
output_xml = generate_custom_fault_response(tool_number)
print(ET.tostring(output_xml).decode('utf-8'))
```

```python
# Generate stubs from WSDL using jdk 17 and gradle 7.6

# Add the following dependencies to your build.gradle file
dependencies {
    implementation 'com.sun.xml.ws:jaxws-rt:2.3.2'
    implementation 'org.gradle:java-gradle-plugin:7.6'
}

# Create a new task to generate stubs from WSDL
task generateStubs(type: Java) {
    def wsdlFile = new File('path/to/your/wsdl_file.wsdl')
    def generatedFolder = new File('build/generated')

    // Create the generated folder if it doesn't exist
    if (!generatedFolder.exists()) {
        generatedFolder.mkdirs()
    }

    // Generate the stubs
    def jaxwsTool = new org.gradle.api.tasks.javax.jaxws.JaxWsTool()
    jaxwsTool.setWsdl(wsdlFile)
    jaxwsTool.setTargetFolder(generatedFolder)
    jaxwsTool.execute()

    // Define the main class
    main = 'com.example.Main'
}

// Create a new class to handle the main method
class Main {
    static void main(String[] args) {
        generateStubs()
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

1.  We first add the required dependencies to the build.gradle file. The `jaxws-rt` dependency is used to generate the stubs, and the `java-gradle-plugin` is used to run the `jaxwsTool` task.
2.  We then create a new task called `generateStubs` that uses the `Java` task type.
3.  Inside the `generateStubs` task, we define the WSDL file path and the generated folder path. We also create the generated folder if it doesn't exist.
4.  We then generate the stubs using the `jaxwsTool` task. The `setWsdl` method is used to specify the WSDL file, and the `setTargetFolder` method is used to specify the generated folder.
5.  Finally, we define a `Main` class with a `main` method that calls the `generateStubs` task.

**Note:** Replace `'path/to/your/wsdl_file.wsdl'` with the actual path to your WSDL file. Also, make sure to adjust the package name in the `Main` class according to your needs.

Questions:
Title: Getting the BaseAddress of an HttpClient service from withing a Blazor page.
Body: <p>I have a Blazor Server app that calls an aspx net core web API. In program.cs I set up the application as an HttpClient service and inject it into my web page. In production, the entire application (both the service and the Blazor app) are delivered on different servers. I need to be able to display the BaseAddress of the web API on a Blazor page - the URL will be different on different servers of course.</p>
<p>If I inject the service into my Blazor page I only get a reference to the functions available in the controller, not the properties of the service itself.  How can I access the info that was specified in Program.cs for the service?</p>
<p>in Index.razor</p>
<pre><code>@code {
[Inject]
public MyAspx myservice { get; set; }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
         var baseurl = myservice.BaseAddress;    // for illustration purposes only this is what i need, not what it will provide
    }
}

in Program.cs:
builder.Services.AddHttpClient&lt;IMyAspx, MyAspx&gt;(client =&gt;
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri(&quot;https://localhost:7132/&quot;);
    client.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
});
</code></pre>

Answers:
```python
# No Python code is required to solve this problem.

# However, here's how you can achieve the same result in C#.

# In Program.cs, you already have the line that sets the BaseAddress of the HttpClient service.
# To access this from your Blazor page, you can directly use the service instance.

<pre>
// In Program.cs
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IMyAspx, MyAspx>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://localhost:7132/");
    client.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
});

// In Index.razor
<pre>
@code {
    [Inject]
    public MyAspx myservice { get; set; }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var baseurl = myservice.BaseAddress.ToString();
    }
}
</pre>
```

