```python
import subprocess
import time
import os

def run_service():
    try:
        subprocess.check_call(['/bin/bash', '-c', '/usr/bin/psql ... -f /path/to/refresh_matviews.sql'])
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:
        print(f"Error: {e}")
        send_email("not ok: refresh matviews")

def send_email(subject):
    subprocess.check_call(['/usr/bin/mail', '-s', subject, 'admin@foo.com'])

def check_failed_log():
    if os.path.exists('refresh_matviews.failed.log'):
        with open('refresh_matviews.failed.log', 'r') as f:
            contents = f.read()
            if 'seen' not in contents:
                send_email(subject="failed!")
                with open('refresh_matviews.failed.log', 'w') as f:
                    f.write('seen')
    else:
        send_email(subject="failed!")

while True:
    run_service()
    check_failed_log()
    time.sleep(300)  # wait 5 minutes
```

Note: This script will run indefinitely until it is manually stopped. It checks every 5 minutes if the SQL script failed and sends an email if necessary. If the email has already been sent, it will not send another one.

# Potential Issues with SendGrid API
### 1. Incorrect API Key or Credentials

```python
import requests
import json

def send_email(api_key, from_email, to_email, subject, body):
    url = 'https://api.sendgrid.com/v3/messages'
    headers = {
        'Authorization': f'Bearer {api_key}',
        'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    }
    data = {
        'message': {
            'subject': subject,
            'body': {
                'text': body,
                'html': body
            },
            'from': {
                'email': from_email
            },
            'to': [
                {
                    'email': to_email
                }
            ]
        }
    }

    try:
        response = requests.post(url, headers=headers, data=json.dumps(data))
        response.raise_for_status()
    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:
        print(f"Request Exception: {e}")
        return False
    else:
        return True

# Example usage
if __name__ == "__main__":
    api_key = 'YOUR_SENDGRID_API_KEY'
    from_email = 'from@example.com'
    to_email = 'to@example.com'
    subject = 'Test Email'
    body = 'This is a test email sent via SendGrid'

    if send_email(api_key, from_email, to_email, subject, body):
        print("Email sent successfully.")
    else:
        print("Failed to send email.")
```

### 2. Rate Limit Exceeded

Ensure that you're not exceeding SendGrid's rate limits, as this can result in IP blacklisting.

### 3. IP Blacklisting

Check if your IP is blacklisted by SendGrid. If it is, you can try whitelisting it.

### 4. Domain Configuration

Verify that your domain is configured correctly in SendGrid. This includes verifying your domain ownership and setting up SPF and DKIM records.

### 5. Missing Domain Keys

If you're using a custom domain, ensure that you have set up domain keys (DKIM and SPF) correctly.

### 6. Missing SSL/TLS Configuration

Ensure that your server has an SSL/TLS certificate configured. This is required for sending emails via SendGrid.

### 7. Incorrect DNS Configuration

Verify that your DNS configuration is correct. This includes verifying that your MX records point to your server.

### 8. Server Configuration

Ensure that your server is configured correctly for sending emails. This includes verifying that your server has the necessary permissions and that your mail server is configured to use SendGrid's SMTP servers.

### 9. Missing Authentication

Ensure that you have properly authenticated with SendGrid using an API key or OAuth.

### 10. Invalid Email Addresses

Verify that the email addresses you're sending to are valid and existent.

Questions:
Title: Azure communication service email sending error.
Body: <p>I created a communication service in azure to send mail. Then I added a verified custom domain. However, when I send an email, I get an error like this;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>RestError: The specified sender domain has not been linked.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am able to send successfully with azure managed domain mail.</p>
<p>Here is my code:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code> const connectionString = `endpoint=https://*******`;
    const client = new EmailClient(connectionString);

    const emailMessage = {
        sender: &quot;noreply@****.com&quot;,
        content: {
            subject: &quot;This is the subject&quot;,
            plainText: &quot;This is the body&quot;,
        },
        recipients: {
            to: [
                {
                    email: &quot;*****&quot;,
                    displayName: &quot;Test test&quot;,
                },
            ],
        },
    };

    const message = await client.send(emailMessage);
</code></pre>
<p>How can I solve this problem ?</p>

Answers:
```python
import requests

def send_email(sender, recipient, subject, body, custom_domain):
    # Set up the Azure Communication Service endpoint and authentication
    endpoint = "https://communication.azure.com/"
    headers = {
        "Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key": "YOUR_SUBSCRIPTION_KEY",
        "Content-Type": "application/json"
    }

    # Set up the email message
    data = {
        "sender": {
            "address": sender,
            "domain": custom_domain
        },
        "recipients": [
            {
                "email": recipient,
                "displayname": "Test test"
            }
        ],
        "subject": subject,
        "content": {
            "type": "text",
            "value": body
        }
    }

    # Send the email using the Azure Communication Service API
    response = requests.post(endpoint + "send", headers=headers, json=data)

    # Check if the email was sent successfully
    if response.status_code == 200:
        print("Email sent successfully")
    else:
        print("Error sending email:", response.text)

# Example usage
connection_string = "endpoint=https://*******"
client = None  # Not needed in Python

custom_domain = "yourcustomdomain.com"
sender = "noreply@yourcustomdomain.com"
recipient = "*****"
subject = "This is the subject"
body = "This is the body"

send_email(sender, recipient, subject, body, custom_domain)
```

This code sends an email using the Azure Communication Service API. It uses the `requests` library to send a POST request to the `/send` endpoint with the email message data. The `Content-Type` header is set to `application/json` to match the expected request body format. The `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key` header is required for authentication with your Azure subscription.

